{
  "abstract": "Tao's almost-all Collatz theorem rests on a decay estimate for the characteristic function $\\chi_n(\\xi)=\\mathbb{E}\\,e(\\xi\\,\\mathrm{Syrac}(\\mathbb{Z}/3^n)/3^n)$ of the Syracuse random variable, proved with polynomial decay $O(n^{-A})$; Tao remarks that a random-map heuristic suggests exponential decay but does not pursue it. We study the true size of $M_n=\\max_{3\\nmid\\xi}|\\chi_n(\\xi)|$. (1) We prove a renewal identity expressing $\\chi_n(2^ju)$ through a cold-walk main term and the lower-level values $\\chi_\\ell(u)$, weighted by the first time the 2-adic walk $s_m$ exceeds $j$. (2) We prove a conditional transfer theorem: if $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|\\le B\\rho^\\ell$ with $\\rho<\\lambda-1\\approx0.585$ ($\\lambda=\\log_23$), then $|\\chi_n(2^j)|\\le C e^{0.3036\\,j}(\\lambda-1)^n$, which is $e^{-In}$ with $I=\\lambda(\\ln2-H(1/\\lambda))\\approx0.05498$ at $j=\\lambda n$. (3) We give an exact $O(n^2)$ algorithm for a single coefficient $\\chi_n(2^ju)$, replacing $O(3^n)$ enumeration and Monte Carlo. With it we compute $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|$ for $\\ell\\le800$: they decay like $e^{-0.565\\ell}$, as random units do (slightly faster than square-root cancellation), and satisfy the hypothesis with $B(0.58)=5.70$. Certified by ball arithmetic up to $\\ell=1000$, this proves the bound of (2) for all $n\\le1000$ and all $j$ (computer-assisted), with $C=39$. (4) We compute $M_n$ exactly for $n\\le18$ and, along the extremal family $\\xi=2^j$, $j\\in[\\lambda n-14,\\lambda n+3]$, up to $n=2000$. Fitted rates are $0.0552$–$0.0553$, while the effective polynomial exponent drifts from $1.0$ to $1.35$ and is not yet determined. Exact multiplier scans at $n=40,80,150$ support that $2^j$ is extremal, and certified lower bounds at $n=100,\\dots,1000$ lie within a factor $1.6\\times10^4$ of the upper bound. These support Conjecture C1, $M_n=e^{-In+O(\\log n)}$, with extremisers concentrated on neutral-growth walks $2^{s_m}\\approx3^m$. This rate is ten times slower than the random-map value $\\tfrac12\\ln3$. Limitations: no unconditional bound for all $n$; for $n>18$ extremality of the family is supported only numerically; the polynomial correction is unsettled; and in Tao's argument the Fourier input is not what limits the density exponent, so exponential decay would not by itself strengthen his theorem.",
